Make the output and the consumed offsets one Kafka transaction. Consumer: group.id=enricher, enable.auto.commit=false, isolation.level=read_committed. Producer: a transactional.id unique per pod, for example enricher-<pod name>. If the pods are a StatefulSet the name also survives a restart, so the restarted pod aborts its predecessor's open transaction; otherwise the coordinator aborts it after transaction.timeout.ms (60 s by default).

producer.initTransactions();
consumer.subscribe(List.of("orders-raw"));
while (running) {
  ConsumerRecords<String,String> recs = consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(500));
  if (recs.isEmpty()) continue;
  try {
    producer.beginTransaction();
    Map<TopicPartition,OffsetAndMetadata> offs = new HashMap<>();
    for (ConsumerRecord<String,String> r : recs) {
      producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("orders-enriched", r.key(), enrich(r.value())));
      offs.put(new TopicPartition(r.topic(), r.partition()), new OffsetAndMetadata(r.offset() + 1));
    }
    producer.sendOffsetsToTransaction(offs, consumer.groupMetadata());
    producer.commitTransaction();
  } catch (ProducerFencedException e) {
    producer.close(); throw e;            // another instance owns the id: stop
  } catch (KafkaException e) {
    producer.abortTransaction();
    Map<TopicPartition,OffsetAndMetadata> c = consumer.committed(recs.partitions());
    for (TopicPartition tp : recs.partitions())   // rewind what the aborted transaction read
      consumer.seek(tp, c.get(tp) != null ? c.get(tp).offset() : recs.records(tp).get(0).offset());
  }
}

No consumer.commitSync(): the offsets commit with the transaction. Billing must read orders-enriched with isolation.level=read_committed; with the default read_uncommitted it still sees records of aborted transactions.

Use only the Admin client; it reads group state without joining the group.

try (Admin admin = Admin.create(props)) {
  ConsumerGroupDescription d;
  try {
    d = admin.describeConsumerGroups(List.of(group)).describedGroups().get(group).get();
  } catch (ExecutionException e) {
    if (e.getCause() instanceof GroupIdNotFoundException) { System.err.println("group " + group + " not found"); System.exit(2); }
    throw e;
  }
  Map<TopicPartition, MemberDescription> owner = new HashMap<>();
  d.members().forEach(m -> m.assignment().topicPartitions().forEach(tp -> owner.put(tp, m)));
  Map<TopicPartition, OffsetAndMetadata> committed = new HashMap<>(admin.listConsumerGroupOffsets(
      Map.of(group, new ListConsumerGroupOffsetsSpec())).partitionsToOffsetAndMetadata(group).get());
  owner.keySet().forEach(tp -> committed.putIfAbsent(tp, null));   // owned, nothing committed yet
  Map<TopicPartition, OffsetSpec> req = new HashMap<>();
  committed.keySet().forEach(tp -> req.put(tp, OffsetSpec.latest()));
  Map<TopicPartition, ListOffsetsResult.ListOffsetsResultInfo> end = admin.listOffsets(req).all().get();
  for (TopicPartition tp : committed.keySet()) {
    OffsetAndMetadata c = committed.get(tp);
    long e = end.get(tp).offset();
    String lag = c == null ? "-" : Long.toString(e - c.offset());
    MemberDescription m = owner.get(tp);
    System.out.printf("%s %d %s %d %s %s %s%n", tp.topic(), tp.partition(), c == null ? "-" : c.offset(), e, lag,
        m == null ? "-" : m.clientId(), m == null ? "-" : m.host());
  }
}

Since Kafka 4.0, describeConsumerGroups throws GroupIdNotFoundException for an unknown group instead of returning a DEAD description. The principal needs Describe on the group (OffsetFetch, DescribeGroups, ConsumerGroupDescribe) and Describe on the topics (OffsetFetch, ListOffsets); nothing else.

Two fixes. ledger-clients-ca-cert holds the clients CA, which brokers use to verify client certificates; broker certificates are signed by the cluster CA in ledger-cluster-ca-cert, hence the PKIX error. And the KafkaUser is scram-sha-512, so SCRAM-SHA-256 would fail next.

Deployment: mount Secret ledger-cluster-ca-cert at /etc/kafka/ca (it contains ca.crt), and set env KAFKA_JAAS from Secret invoice-writer, key sasl.jaas.config, with valueFrom.secretKeyRef. Both Secrets are in fin, like the service.

Properties p = new Properties();
p.put("bootstrap.servers", "ledger-kafka-bootstrap:9093");
p.put("security.protocol", "SASL_SSL");
p.put("sasl.mechanism", "SCRAM-SHA-512");
p.put("sasl.jaas.config", System.getenv("KAFKA_JAAS"));
p.put("ssl.truststore.type", "PEM");
p.put("ssl.truststore.location", "/etc/kafka/ca/ca.crt");
p.put("key.serializer", "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer");
p.put("value.serializer", "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer");
try (KafkaProducer<String, String> producer = new KafkaProducer<>(p)) {
    producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("invoices", key, value));
}

The User Operator keeps that Secret current, so the password never appears in code, image or manifest. Leave ssl.endpoint.identification.algorithm at its default (https).

The old version was silent because a STREAM_TIME punctuator is driven by record timestamps: when no heartbeats arrive, stream time does not advance and punctuate() is never called. Use PunctuationType.WALL_CLOCK_TIME. transform() and Transformer were removed in 4.0; use KStream#process with a Processor.

StoreBuilder<KeyValueStore<String, Long>> lastSeen = Stores.keyValueStoreBuilder(
    Stores.persistentKeyValueStore("last-seen"), Serdes.String(), Serdes.Long());
builder.addStateStore(lastSeen);
builder.stream("device-heartbeats", Consumed.with(Serdes.String(), Serdes.String()))
       .process(OfflineDetector::new, "last-seen")
       .to("device-offline", Produced.with(Serdes.String(), Serdes.String()));

class OfflineDetector implements Processor<String, String, String, String> {
  private KeyValueStore<String, Long> store;
  public void init(ProcessorContext<String, String> ctx) {
    store = ctx.getStateStore("last-seen");
    ctx.schedule(Duration.ofSeconds(10), PunctuationType.WALL_CLOCK_TIME, now -> {
      try (KeyValueIterator<String, Long> it = store.all()) {
        while (it.hasNext()) {
          KeyValue<String, Long> e = it.next();
          if (e.value >= 0 && now - e.value > 60_000) {
            ctx.forward(new Record<>(e.key, "offline", now));
            store.put(e.key, -1L);   // alerted; reset by the next heartbeat
          }
        }
      }
    });
  }
  public void process(Record<String, String> r) {
    store.put(r.key(), System.currentTimeMillis());
  }
}

OfflineDetector::new gives each task its own processor. The punctuator's argument is wall-clock time, so last-seen is stored as wall-clock time too.

correct_alternative:
Rewrite with the Processor API and wall-clock punctuation. The 3.6 code scheduled with STREAM_TIME, which only advances when records are processed, so with no heartbeats the check never ran; and Transformer/transform() are gone in 4.x.

class OfflineDetector implements Processor<String, String, String, String> {
  private KeyValueStore<String, Long> seen;
  private ProcessorContext<String, String> context;
  @Override public void init(ProcessorContext<String, String> context) {
    this.context = context;
    seen = context.getStateStore("seen");
    context.schedule(Duration.ofSeconds(5), PunctuationType.WALL_CLOCK_TIME, this::sweep);
  }
  private void sweep(long now) {
    try (KeyValueIterator<String, Long> it = seen.all()) {
      while (it.hasNext()) {
        KeyValue<String, Long> kv = it.next();
        if (now - kv.value > 60_000) {
          context.forward(new Record<>(kv.key, "offline", now));
          seen.delete(kv.key);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  @Override public void process(Record<String, String> r) { seen.put(r.key(), System.currentTimeMillis()); }
}

Wire it with builder.addStateStore(Stores.keyValueStoreBuilder(Stores.persistentKeyValueStore("seen"), Serdes.String(), Serdes.Long())) and stream.process(OfflineDetector::new, "seen").to("device-offline").
